From: Caboose <caboose@local>
Subject: [PATCH 77/91] Give every dispatchable object its executor generation

A process outlives its executor: the phone leaves and returns, and the process connects to
the new executor with its next instance. Its old objects did not go with the old executor:
once another thread had reconnected, a call on a pre-loss device, queue, command buffer or
physical device was encoded to the new executor, which does not know its handles (an
unboxing failure ends the executor) or knows them as other objects. Each dispatchable object
now records the executor generation it was made under (caboose::completion::
connectionGeneration, one more for each executor after a loss), and every encoder entry
reports VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST for an object of an earlier generation without touching the
stream; the application rebuilds on the new executor.

Answers and tables of the previous executor that were kept for the process are forgotten on
the new generation through one reset hook (caboose::completion::onNewGeneration): the cached
memory types, heaps and budget, the instance extension table, the device features the driver
emulates around, the devices' presentation queues, completion watches owed to old queues
and host allocations shared through dma-bufs. A device's presentation queue is forgotten
with the device.

A device whose executor is gone answers mapping as a lost Vulkan device must (vkMapMemory never
reports device loss), and memory allocated while it is lost is local zeroed memory that maps
(caboose-bounded-memory/lost_memory.h): Zink, uploading a texture when its executor left, got
VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST from vkMapMemory and crashed (the compositor with it). Flushing and
invalidating a lost device's memory succeed with nothing to do.

Every command whose specification has no VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST gives a result it allows on a
lost device: physical-device enumeration fails to initialize, image-format queries report the
format unsupported, other enumerations are empty, semaphore and fence exports are signaled
(-1), host image copies and layout transitions succeed. A descriptor pool whose set ids the
executor never sent makes its sets locally, and a pool out of ids reports
VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_POOL_MEMORY instead of taking an id it does not have: Zink, allocating a
descriptor set when its executor left, got VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST and crashed. A lost device's
command pools keep their staging until they are destroyed: resetting each of Zink's piled-up
pools walked every pool's command buffers and held the compositor for tens of seconds.

Where a call on a lost device reports the loss, it gives caboose::completion::lostResult():
VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST, or VK_SUCCESS for a process launched with CABOOSE_GPU_ON_LOSS=quiet (the
compositor), whose lost device completes everything and whose timeline semaphores have reached
every value, so Zink never takes its device-loss paths.
